---
import ToolShell from '../../components/ToolShell.astro';
import { u } from '../../utils/url';
---

<ToolShell
  slug="ir-temperature-correction-calculator"
  title="Insulation Resistance Temperature Correction Calculator"
  description="Free calculator: correct a megger insulation resistance reading to 20°C or 40°C using the 10°C halving rule, so readings taken on different days can be compared."
  h1="Insulation Resistance Temperature Correction Calculator"
  intro="Enter your megger reading and the winding temperature. Get the reading corrected to a common reference temperature so you can trend it honestly."
  productLabel="View Insulation Testers"
  productHref="/products/insulation-testers-hand"
  related={[
    { href: '/tools/pi-dar-calculator', label: 'PI and DAR Calculator' },
    { href: '/tools/motor-minimum-insulation-resistance', label: 'Motor Minimum Insulation Resistance Check' },
  ]}
>
  <div slot="calculator">
    <div class="tool-grid">
      <div class="tool-field"><label for="ir">Measured insulation resistance</label>
        <input id="ir" type="number" min="0" step="any" value="25" inputmode="decimal" /></div>
      <div class="tool-field"><label for="unit">Unit</label>
        <select id="unit"><option value="1">MΩ</option><option value="1000">GΩ</option><option value="0.001">kΩ</option></select></div>
      <div class="tool-field"><label for="temp">Winding temperature at test (°C)</label>
        <input id="temp" type="number" min="-20" max="150" step="any" value="40" inputmode="decimal" /></div>
      <div class="tool-field"><label for="ref">Correct to</label>
        <select id="ref"><option value="20">20°C (common for trending)</option><option value="40">40°C (IEEE 43 reference)</option></select></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tool-result" aria-live="polite">
      <div class="big" id="out">-</div>
      <div class="sub" id="detail"></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>Insulation resistance falls as temperature rises. The field rule, used in IEEE 43 practice, is that the reading roughly halves for every 10°C rise and doubles for every 10°C fall. The correction factor is:</p>
  <p class="font-mono text-sm bg-cloud rounded-lg p-3">Factor = 2 ^ ((T<sub>test</sub> - T<sub>ref</sub>) / 10)  ·  Corrected IR = Measured IR × Factor</p>
  <p>Example: 25 MΩ measured at 40°C is 100 MΩ when referred to 20°C (factor 4.0). Log the corrected value, not the raw one.</p>
  <h2>Limits of this calculation</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It is an approximation. The true factor varies with insulation class and moisture, so use it for trending and screening, not for a legal pass/fail.</li>
    <li>Take the actual winding temperature, not the room temperature. A surface or infrared thermometer on the frame is a reasonable stand-in.</li>
    <li>Do not use it for PI or DAR. Those are ratios of readings in one test at one temperature, so temperature cancels out. Use the <a href={u('/tools/pi-dar-calculator/')}>PI and DAR calculator</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Background reading: <a href={u('/blog/insulation-resistance-temperature-correction/')}>Insulation resistance temperature correction explained</a>.</p>

  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      var $ = function (id) { return document.getElementById(id); };
      function fmt(v) { return v >= 1000 ? (v / 1000).toPrecision(4) + ' GΩ' : v.toPrecision(4) + ' MΩ'; }
      function calc() {
        var ir = parseFloat($('ir').value) * parseFloat($('unit').value);
        var t = parseFloat($('temp').value), ref = parseFloat($('ref').value);
        if (!isFinite(ir) || !isFinite(t) || ir < 0) { $('out').textContent = '-'; $('detail').textContent = 'Enter valid numbers.'; return; }
        var f = Math.pow(2, (t - ref) / 10);
        $('out').textContent = fmt(ir * f) + ' at ' + ref + '°C';
        $('detail').textContent = 'Correction factor ' + f.toPrecision(4) + ' (measured ' + fmt(ir) + ' at ' + t + '°C)';
      }
      ['ir', 'unit', 'temp', 'ref'].forEach(function (id) { $(id).addEventListener('input', calc); });
      calc();
    })();
  </script>
</ToolShell>
